  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nEarly this morning, upper air analysis shows a weak ridge axis \nextending from southern California to the central plains over \nthe top of cutoff low meandering over northern Mexico. At the \nsurface, a very weak frontal boundary is sagging into northern \nKansas with winds on either side of this boundary remaining at \nor below 5 mph. Throughout the day, this frontal boundary will \nwash out, and mostly sunny skies should allow for temperatures \nto warm up into the upper 60s. By tonight, clear skies, and \nanother weak surface ridge promoting calm winds will allow for \noptimal radiational cooling across the region. As a result, \nanother bout of patchy fog appears increasingly likely late \ntonight into early Monday morning, especially across south- \ncentral and southeast Kansas. \n\nGuidance continues to show a hint of weak mid/upper ridging \nremaining over the central plains for Monday. This along with mostly \nsunny skies and light southerly low-level flow should allow for \ntemperatures to climb into the low 70s across the area. The \nfirst of a series of weak frontal passages will arrive late \nMonday night or early Tuesday morning, but this will do little \nto impact afternoon temperatures on Tuesday with highs forecast \nto be in the mid 60s to low 70s. A relatively stronger cold \nfront will pass through the area on Wednesday, and a brief cool \ndown is forecast for Wednesday and Thursday with highs in the \n60s to potentially the upper 50s. Mid to long range guidance is \nsignaling a more amplified pattern late this week and into next\nweekend with fairly decent mid/upper ridging across the central\nplains. This will generally support the prospect of another \nmodest warming trend with highs returning to the 70s by the \nweekend.",
